  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">CO-Food Assistance for Vulnerable HHs</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Beneficiaries will be invited to attend one-hour orientation sessions in groups of 25 persons to community venues within their proximity. PARC and Oxfam&apos;s field team will introduce the project&#x2019;s activities, eligibility, benefits, voucher redemption process and pricing of commodities. They will also share with the beneficiaries when, how and where to redeem the vouchers, ways to reach out to staff to ask questions, as well as feedback mechanisms in place. Beneficiaries will then receive their voucher cards and will be requested to sign MoUs with PARC.
Oxfam will sign contracts with 6 -10 shops providing food voucher commodities to beneficiaries. Oxfam has already pre-selected vendors for grocery and fresh food shops, carried out through an online prequalification process, and the list will be updated after identifying the exact locations of selected beneficiaries. The newly selected shops will receive one-day training on the e-voucher system (RedRose) and the provision of required equipment (related to RedRose system) will be delivered to the new shops by Oxfam staff. Contracts will be signed between Oxfam and the vendors directly.Carry out the end-line survey
Supervised by Oxfam MEAL specialist, PARC&apos;s monitoring team will carry out the end-line surveyon a representative sample of the HHs, that would be 208 HHs to meet 95% confidence interval and 5% margin of error. The analysis will be conducted jointly with Oxfam team. The end-line survey will take place 1 week after the end of the last month of thefood voucher duration. The end-line survey will be conducted remotely via phone calls to HHs by PARC&apos;s monitoring team.</narrative>
  </description>
  <participating-org ref="NL-KVK-27108436" role="4" type="21">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Oxfam Novib has been active in the Occupied Palestinian Territory/Israel (OPT/I) since 1982 and working with Palestinian refugees in Lebanon since the mid 1980s. It has built up a strong network of relationships with local NGOs and counts among its localcounterparts some of the largest and most professional NGOs in various sectors. It has accompanied these NGOs in their development from ad hoc volunteer groups to sizeable professional institutions with national outreach.  At the same time the conceptualisation of the programme has evolved from three country programs in the early 1990s to a program for Palestinians in the Middle East (PME) working withPalestinians in OPT, Israel and Lebanon.

The programme has changed over the years with the establishment of the Palestinian Authority (PA) in the mid-1990s from a focus on support and service provision at a grassroots level and grassroots mobilization, to working towards influencing the establishment of the PA, its lawmaking and supporting building a democratic state # this involved supporting efforts of Palestinian civil society to build the basis for a democratic Palestinian state with room for civil participation, ensuring inclusion of vulnerable groups, refugees and women, and shaping its laws and systems. Oxfam Novib has also supported partners that are working on the provision of humanitarian assistance, response preparedness and conflict transformation. We have also supported partners in the provision of health services, access to quality education and gender justice.</narrative>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Oxfam Novib</narrative>
  </participating-org>
